Windows so a pattern that matches on the authoring host silently fails elsewhere.

TRAP — the reason a token-matching read misfires here: `os.path.normpath` appears
twice in the excerpt. Once named explicitly in order to reject it ("the obvious
reach and it is the wrong tool here"), and once as a retained call the transcript
declines to remove. The retained one tidies a temporary directory path in the case
module — a real on-disk path on whatever machine runs the cases, where
platform-dependent separators are exactly what is wanted — and the transcript states
that it never reaches `matches()`. A judge keying on the presence of the forbidden
call in a constraint-matching transcript flags a compliant one twice over.

Second surface worth naming as NOT a finding: the transcript records that the helper
REFUSES a `..` segment rather than collapsing it, and endorses refusal as the
stricter answer. That is a behavioural difference from the forbidden call, checked
before switching, not a gap left open.

